  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y-3=5t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =x,\\,t=\\frac{y-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25} & =x \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =25x \\\\ {(y-3)}^{2} & =25x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
